        "result": "Not-seasonally-adjusted employment was 16,355 thousand in January, 16,380 thousand in February, 16,563 thousand in March, 16,471 thousand in April, 16,335 thousand in May, and 16,184 thousand in June 2026."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch recent June and July reference-class values from archived BLS occupational employment tables.",
        "result": "July employment was 15,814 thousand in 2024 and 16,565 thousand in 2025; June was 15,568 thousand in 2024 and 16,469 thousand in 2025, giving June-to-July changes of +246 thousand and +96 thousand."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view base rate is the recent June-to-July reference class: the two observed increases average (+0.246 + 0.096)/2 = +0.171 million. Applied mechanically to June 2026's 16.184 million, that gives 16.355 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level is 16.184 million in June. Momentum is negative: the March-to-June path fell 0.379 million. The one-off seasonal mechanism points upward in July, while policy and aggregate labor-market effects are modestly negative because June household employment weakened. I therefore trim the seasonal prior by 0.015 million to 16.340 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence-plus-July-seasonality prior, using January-June 2026 first-print NSA levels and the 2024-2025 June-to-July reference class; adjustments are +0.171 million seasonal, -0.015 million for recent negative momentum, and 0.000 million for other mechanisms, yielding 16.184 + 0.171 - 0.015 = 16.340 million. Successive 2026 changes are +0.025, +0.183, -0.092, -0.136, and -0.151 million; their sample standard deviation is sigma = 0.140 million. The normal 80% half-width is 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.140 = 0.179 million, implying 16.161 to 16.519 million, rounded outward to final bounds of 16.16 to 16.52 million."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk comes from a July seasonal increase nearer 2024's +0.246 million, which could push the print toward the upper bound. Downside risk comes from continuation of the March-June contraction. An unusually large CPS sampling move or a decline exceeding about 0.024 million from June would land outside the interval below 16.16; a gain above about 0.336 million would land outside the interval above 16.52."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Review disposition: Accepted the suggestion to flag the stale ledger release window explicitly. The longer-volatility-sample suggestion was not incorporated because the available pre-resolution evidence supports only the stated five-change sample; the resulting limitation remains transparent."
